The edit distance between two strings is the minimum number of operations required to transform one string into the other.
The allowed operations are:
Add one character to the string.
Remove one character from the string.
Replace one character in the string.
For example, the edit distance between LOVE and MOVIE is 2, because you can first replace L with M, and then add I.
Your task is to calculate the edit distance between two strings.

Input:
The first input line has a string that contains n characters between A–Z.
The second input line has a string that contains m characters between A–Z.

Output:
Print one integer: the edit distance between the strings.

Constraints:
1<=n,m<=5000

Strategy:
This is 2d dp. The trick is that at dp[i][j] we are measuring how hard is it to get the prefix of length i of string 1 into the prefix of string 2 of length j. This is useful since for the transitions we know that if s1[i]==s2[j] the letters for those prefixes match and the add is free. Otherwise we just take the lowest count possible which is applicable to this which is either dp[i-1][j]+1 for adding a letter to s1 to match s2 of length j or dp[i][j-1]+1 for adding a letter to s2 which amounts to removing from s1 since if i need to add a letter to the target i need to remove from the starting one. Or we have dp[i-1][j-1]+1 which is replacement. This signifies that both s1 and s2 have a letter at i and j but they dont match so we change the letter in s1 to the corresponding one in s2. Since i now have strings that match in the last letter i look at how much would it cost to get the rest of them. This is saved in dp[i-1][j-1] and the cost for dp[i][j] is this +1. We take the minimum of these operations. We iterate like this through the whole 2d array and output dp[n][m]. We initialize dp with INF everywhere except the topmost row and leftmost column. These will have dp[0][i]=i and dp[j][0]=j since the cost to create a string from nothing is all its letters and cost to make nothing from a string is the length of the string.